< https://github.com/tacplus-dev/gateway/issues/6#issuecomment-6446155806 https://github.com/tacplus-dev/gateway/issues/6
I stopped issue #6 without committing anything. I posted a question on the issue with my recommendation and added the `needs-decision` label.

**Why it can't be built as written:** tacplus#180 has fleets run each service's published release image. The gateway's image isn't the same for every fleet, so one release image can't serve them all:
- **Route table:** `grpc-servers: all` compiles the routes from whichever proto tree the gateway is built against. Any gRPC path not in that table is rejected (`src/proxy_grpc.rs:350`). A release image built from the registry would reject calls to services or contract versions a fleet adds after that release.
- **Web console (`webapp-dist/`):** it's built against the fleet's merged protos.
- **Other contents:** the customer web app (`web-dist/`), the client binaries (`clients/`) and the launcher binary are all supplied by the fleet.

**Options I put in the comment:**
- **A. Release against the registry:** the tag build uses the registry as it is at the tag. Any contract change anywhere in the fleet then needs a new gateway release before fleets can route it.
- **B. Make the gateway the exception (my recommendation):** tag `vX.Y.Z`, cut GitHub releases, and fleets keep building the gateway image themselves from the released tag. This is how fleet-seatrials builds it today.
- **C. Load the route table at runtime (follow-up):** read it from the fleet's protos instead of compiling it in, so one image serves every fleet. That's a real gateway change and would be its own issue.

**Also needed:** for A or B, the release workflow will need an org secret. GitHub's built-in token can't read the private registry repo, the compiler image or the launcher.

Once someone picks an option and supplies the secret, I'll follow the nsproxy release:
- the workflow;
- tag and release-notes scripts with fixture tests;
- a README "Releasing" section;
- tagging `v0.1.0`.
